00:16:09,360 --> 00:16:11,200
But back then it was like literally like a Hill Mary.

206
00:16:11,360 --> 00:16:12,160
We think this makes sense.

207
00:16:12,320 --> 00:16:12,880
Let's give it a try.

208
00:16:13,280 --> 00:16:13,920
And there was no data.

209
00:16:14,080 --> 00:16:18,080
There was no insight into what works, what doesn't work, what's what are good numbers and what's not.

210
00:16:18,160 --> 00:16:20,720
And so there were, there were two problems in our initial launch.

211
00:16:20,960 --> 00:16:23,120
The first was that our prices were too low.

212
00:16:23,280 --> 00:16:24,000
We offered too much.

213
00:16:24,400 --> 00:16:25,440
The cognitive load was high.

214
00:16:25,920 --> 00:16:28,480
You didn't actually know that there were things to buy in the app.

215
00:16:28,800 --> 00:16:30,640
So we were very not pushy about it.

216
00:16:30,640 --> 00:16:33,440
And so people would just get confused about what's free and what's not free.

217
00:16:33,920 --> 00:16:46,000
The biggest problem, the bigger problem was we didn't, uh, I didn't realize how much filters and filter packs were a brand lifestyle marketing product, more so an aspirational product, more so than a functional product.

218
00:16:46,320 --> 00:16:49,280
And so I was treating filters as like, oh, they make your photo look good.

219
00:16:49,360 --> 00:16:51,920
But like, that's not at all what filters and photos are about.

220
00:16:52,400 --> 00:16:56,400
They're about making my photos look like these famous people I follow on Instagram.

221
00:16:56,720 --> 00:17:04,320
And so, like, you needed to get those influencers on Instagram talking about the M five filter in VSCO, getting everyone excited about it, then going to adopt it.

222
00:17:04,480 --> 00:17:10,720
And I was taking a very functional approach, a functional mentality to it when it was an aspirational mentality that I needed to take in.

223
00:17:10,880 --> 00:17:23,040
And I didn't really appreciate that I needed to spend years and lots of dollars investing in creating this high touch brand and marketing and hire these influencers to create content for me and like invest in the brand over time.

224
00:17:23,360 --> 00:17:26,240
Our approach to mobile photography has been very functional in nature.

225
00:17:26,880 --> 00:17:31,440
And in the first few years of Darkroom we've taken, that wasn't clear to us.

226
00:17:31,600 --> 00:17:33,360
And so we were trying things and they weren't working.

227
00:17:33,680 --> 00:17:38,160
And I think we're more mature about all of those things now, but that was the problem.

228
00:17:39,040 --> 00:18:00,240
Well, one thing that's really interesting there and I wanted to touch on is that maybe a third problem too is, and I've been hearing this more and more from developers who have a very multiprong strategy with like a lot of advertising and organics and everything, is that from what I've heard from a lot of different developers and seen in my own apps is that.

229
00:18:00,240 --> 00:18:08,400
that when Apple features your app, it's great and you get a lot of downloads, but those are often very low intent users.

230
00:18:09,120 --> 00:18:11,920
So, like, have you, have you seen that kind of play out?

231
00:18:12,080 --> 00:18:20,720
Because like someone who's like searching the app store, like, I need a new photo app has just a really different, like, perspective than it's like, oh, Apple's featuring Darkroom.

232
00:18:20,880 --> 00:18:21,440
Maybe it's cool.

233
00:18:21,600 --> 00:18:22,480
Maybe I'll check it out.

234
00:18:22,640 --> 00:18:32,640
Maybe whatever versus someone who's like searching, like, you know, raw, you know, photo editor and like, you know, searching kind of those more tools.

235
00:18:32,720 --> 00:18:36,560
And one of the frustrating things is like we still don't have any way to understand.

236
00:18:36,880 --> 00:18:56,800
understand that through App Store Analytics, like you get a browser, you know, for those of you who don't look at this day in and day out like some of us do, it's like you go into App Store Analytics and you can see a percentage of your users who come from browse and a percentage who come from search and you don't know if they're searching like raw this or like photo editing that or even what browse means, right?

237
00:18:56,960 --> 00:18:59,440
Because browse can be an editorial or it could be a category.

238
00:18:59,760 --> 00:19:00,400
We have no idea.

239
00:19:00,640 --> 00:19:00,720
Yeah.

240
00:19:00,960 --> 00:19:01,040
Yeah.

241
00:19:01,280 --> 00:19:02,240
No, it's totally fair.

242
00:19:02,400 --> 00:19:12,800
I mean, totally to your point, like, um, there's no attribution of where the traffic came from like outside of the App Store, but then it's literally browser search, which is like, we don't know if it was an editorial.

243
00:19:13,120 --> 00:19:16,560
or today's story or a tile or a carousel, like, no idea.

244
00:19:17,040 --> 00:19:24,800
And like, for a while, I manually tried to track it so as to have an understanding of, like, why the hell do we see this peak today?

245
00:19:25,120 --> 00:19:25,600
What happened?

246
00:19:25,760 --> 00:19:28,080
Oh, oh, there was a, like, a feature in Canada.

247
00:19:28,240 --> 00:19:28,640
Okay, great.

248
00:19:29,280 --> 00:19:37,120
Um, but, but it's totally fair to say that, like, conversion generally tends to be, like, poorer when there's a feature of that kind.

249
00:19:37,440 --> 00:19:37,840
We see it.

250
00:19:38,000 --> 00:19:40,160
We kind of see a conversion dip a little bit.

251
00:19:40,320 --> 00:19:42,320
So conversion being like download to purchase.

252
00:19:42,720 --> 00:19:44,960
Uh, so you, yeah, you get a you get a peak in downloads.

253
00:19:45,200 --> 00:19:45,360
Great.

254
00:19:45,520 --> 00:19:52,640
But then, like, for us, it's like, yeah, downloads aren't really the currency we care about, it's really like does it convert into a purchase or a subscription these days?

255
00:19:53,120 --> 00:19:55,200
Um, you definitely see a bump there.

256
00:19:55,360 --> 00:19:56,880
And then the opposite is true too.

257
00:19:57,040 --> 00:20:09,840
It's like when like someone creates an awesome YouTube video or like, you know, like an influencer type photographer posts something on on Instagram, we'll see the in, like the opposite, like like, like almost every download converts to a purchase.

258
00:20:10,080 --> 00:20:10,160
Yeah.

259
00:20:10,720 --> 00:20:17,920
You know, like Apple featuring you in and of itself might not always be the most valuable, uh, just from a conversion perspective.

260
00:20:18,480 --> 00:20:25,360
But when you think about it from a like legitimizing you as a company or product perspective, it is extremely useful, right?

261
00:20:26,160 --> 00:20:40,640
It's like if Apple year over year keeps featuring you and you're in the editor's pick and you're in the top app store of the year and now you have an ADA, you know, press bloggers, photographers, they can't really like ignore you.

262
00:20:41,280 --> 00:20:41,360
Yeah.

263
00:20:42,320 --> 00:20:44,720
What did that, what did that post period look like, right?

264
00:20:44,880 --> 00:20:50,320
So we I've I've been through this cycle myself, but you have this pop, you have this like press land.

265
00:20:51,200 --> 00:20:58,880
And then when those, admittedly probably lower intent downloads kind of fade away, you're left with like the organics.

266
00:20:59,360 --> 00:21:00,640
And where did that leave you??

267
00:21:00,720 --> 00:21:05,040
So you had this like maybe too complicated business model, maybe you had some like missed strategies on that stuff.

268
00:21:05,360 --> 00:21:09,680
But then it's 2017 or wherever, wherever you are, posts like all this pop.

269
00:21:10,080 --> 00:21:12,960
Like, what, what, what, what, what looked like on the other side for you guys?

270
00:21:13,440 --> 00:21:23,600
And did, did you see like the early product market fit from the free users, even if the like upgrades weren't to give you some like more quantitative measures of what that looked like for us.

271
00:21:23,840 --> 00:21:25,840
I mean, we crossed a million downloads really quickly.

272
00:21:25,920 --> 00:21:27,440
Like it was, we were, I mean, we were everywhere.

273
00:21:27,520 --> 00:21:28,240
We jumped a million.

274
00:21:28,560 --> 00:21:31,680
I think we made like sixty thousand dollars from that launch.

275
00:21:32,000 --> 00:21:35,040
And then it dipped down to, I mean, we're talking like nothing.

276
00:21:35,360 --> 00:21:40,080
Like just, I mean, it like in 141 or something, it was nothing.

277
00:21:40,320 --> 00:21:47,440
Yeah, we made we made another, we made another 60,000, I think, and it like in the 12 months post launch.

278
00:21:47,680 --> 00:21:48,720
So like literally nothing.

279
00:21:49,200 --> 00:21:54,320
I mean, it's not nothing if it's a side project and you're like augmenting your income, but I was not making money.

280
00:21:55,280 --> 00:21:57,760
I was living in San Francisco for something.

281
00:21:58,000 --> 00:21:59,600
Even the V one of Darkroom, right?

282
00:21:59,840 --> 00:22:01,120
Like that's not great, right?

283
00:22:01,360 --> 00:22:03,440
Like something that quality and invested in it.

284
00:22:03,680 --> 00:22:06,640
And Apple has said like this is valuable and you should download it, right?

285
00:22:07,200 --> 00:22:08,720
Uh, to get that kind of return.

286
00:22:09,040 --> 00:22:11,360
Yeah, we were two people working on it, not making a salary.

287
00:22:11,600 --> 00:22:17,280
So we needed to see at leastast double that for like one of us to be able to survive in San Francisco.

288
00:22:17,840 --> 00:22:19,280
To be able to convince your partners, right?

289
00:22:19,520 --> 00:22:19,600
Yeah.

290
00:22:20,080 --> 00:22:20,160
Yeah.

291
00:22:20,400 --> 00:22:20,720
Keep going.

292
00:22:20,800 --> 00:22:20,880
Yeah.

293
00:22:20,960 --> 00:22:22,800
And so that was one of the problems we dealt with.

294
00:22:22,880 --> 00:22:27,200
But then, honestly, what happened is the numbers were so low I stopped working on Darkroom.

295
00:22:27,440 --> 00:22:28,480
I just literally gave up on it.

296
00:22:28,800 --> 00:22:32,080
For all of 2016, for those twelve months, I touched no code.

297
00:22:32,560 --> 00:22:33,680
And that's when I went to Europe.

298
00:22:33,920 --> 00:22:35,680
I documented the Syrian refugee crisis.

299
00:22:35,760 --> 00:22:39,120
I spent that time working on that book, writing that book, editing it, revising it.

300
00:22:39,920 --> 00:22:43,760
And Jasper was actually helping me with that because Jasper is like a cat with nine lives.

301
00:22:44,320 --> 00:22:47,920
And that's not the right expression because it's like, Oh, that's not the right one.

302
00:22:48,960 --> 00:22:52,320
But like he also worked in print and like in typesetting before.

303
00:22:52,480 --> 00:22:54,640
So he was giving me a lot of feedback on the production of the book.

304
00:22:54,800 --> 00:22:58,320
But again, listen to the launch podcast to get the story on that.

305
00:22:58,800 --> 00:23:03,840
But what ended up happening was I needed to find a job because at this point it had been like two years I didn't earn a living.

306
00:23:04,160 --> 00:23:12,160
And Jasper had finally quit his job at Facebook and we were we had mentioned throughout the past previous couple of years at that point, wanting to work with each other.

307
00:23:12,560 --> 00:23:18,160
And so me and Jasper were just like meeting up, like sharing a glass of wine at a bar in San Francisco and just talking about what we wanted to do.

308
00:23:18,320 --> 00:23:20,240
And Jasper was like, well, I still use Darkroom.

309
00:23:20,400 --> 00:23:22,320
And like, a lot of my friends still use Darkroom.

310
00:23:22,480 --> 00:23:32,400
So David, to answer your question around product market fit like everyone who used darkroom from day one that I knew in my circle continued using it long after I stopped working on it.

311
00:23:32,800 --> 00:23:33,680
The promise was there.

312
00:23:34,080 --> 00:23:34,880
I had the same experience.

313
00:23:35,040 --> 00:23:41,840
Like I remember anyone who was semi serious about taking photos around that same era saying, Oh yeah, darkroom, right?

314
00:23:41,920 --> 00:23:44,320
Like everyone was Yeah, I don't think I appreciated that.

315
00:23:44,400 --> 00:23:47,200
I think if I like, I think I was just depressed a little bit at that point.

316
00:23:47,520 --> 00:23:49,280
Well, it's hard when you're not making money, right?

317
00:23:50,000 --> 00:23:53,360
Like it's hard to appreciate it until people are handing over coal and dollars.

318
00:23:53,920 --> 00:24:03,280
And the thing is there was like there was one fundamental truth for me which is all the motivations for wanting to start working on darkroom in 2014 were true when I and Jasper were having this conversation in 2016, nothing changed.

319
00:24:03,680 --> 00:24:15,440
And I could not live with not giving it a serious next try because what ended up happening was I pulled up App Store Connect and we were still making like seventy thousand dollars a year after not touching the app for like all.

320
00:24:15,760 --> 00:24:24,560
And so I was like, okay, Jasper, Jasper's point was like, okay, you're making if an app didn't make sense and didn't need to exist, it wouldn't make seventy thousand dollars a year on inertia.

321
00:24:24,880 --> 00:24:25,760
Like it would die out.

322
00:24:26,160 --> 00:24:29,680
And so, and we, I think at that point, Jasper, we had like two hundred fifty thousand MAUs.

323
00:24:29,760 --> 00:24:31,920
Like we had some multi-hundred thousand MAUs.

324
00:24:32,000 --> 00:24:32,640
No, no, no, no.

325
00:24:32,720 --> 00:24:33,200
It was more.

326
00:24:33,280 --> 00:24:35,680
It was like four hundred fifty, four hundred fifty thousand or something like.

327
00:24:35,920 --> 00:24:38,240
It was like, what was quite substantial.

328
00:24:38,560 --> 00:24:41,360
That's great for not having growth team push notifications.

329
00:24:41,600 --> 00:24:41,920
Nothing, nothing.

330
00:24:42,160 --> 00:24:43,360
Like, all that, nothing, right?

331
00:24:43,600 --> 00:24:44,160
None of that stuff.

332
00:24:44,880 --> 00:24:46,880
Well, we still barely have any of that, but yeah.

333
00:24:48,160 --> 00:24:53,200
So then the answer to my question is like, it really showed strong product market fit.

334
00:24:53,440 --> 00:24:58,320
I mean, you don't just accidentally, David, have 450,000 monthly active users.

335
00:24:58,800 --> 00:25:01,760
Like, that's a very strong sign of product market fit.

336
00:25:01,920 --> 00:25:03,520
You had amazing retention, right?

337
00:25:03,680 --> 00:25:05,280
Like, that's amazing retention.

338
00:25:05,520 --> 00:25:05,600
Yeah.

339
00:25:05,760 --> 00:25:05,840
Yeah.

340
00:25:06,160 --> 00:25:07,120
Like, let me put it this way.

341
00:25:07,280 --> 00:25:10,480
We were using heap, like heap analytics at the time, and I was friends with the founder.

342
00:25:10,560 --> 00:25:11,680
So he gave me this, like, entry.

343
00:25:11,920 --> 00:25:22,240
He gave me the enterprise plan, but at their introduction tier price, which is really nice of him, except by the end of 2016, he was like, dude, it's costing me like twelve thousand dollars just to process your data.

344
00:25:22,480 --> 00:25:23,360
Like, we need to stop.

345
00:25:23,840 --> 00:25:26,240
Like, we need to stop your volume.

346
00:25:26,640 --> 00:25:28,800
And I was like, that's literally a quarter of my business.

347
00:25:29,200 --> 00:25:30,800
If I pay you that, I'm done.

348
00:25:31,360 --> 00:25:33,120
So like, nothing about Darkroom made any sense.

349
00:25:33,200 --> 00:25:38,880
And the thing for you to realize that kind of influenced our thinking was we came from Instagram and Facebook.

350
00:25:39,440 --> 00:25:45,200
And so our points of reference were completely skewed to what we thought was like successful or relevant.

351
00:25:45,360 --> 00:25:55,920
And so like we, I felt like Darkroom was going nowhere and like nobody was using it when now in hindsight it's like, oh, like, like, you said, like there's a strong signal that there's something worth pursuing here.

352
00:25:56,640 --> 00:26:02,080
But in any case, Jasper had the insight, and I credit Jasper for like reviving Darkroom as a company.

353
00:26:02,160 --> 00:26:03,680
He's like, listen, let's spend a year.

354
00:26:03,840 --> 00:26:09,920
Let's do like the basic things people who run businesses do, which is measure your, measure your, what's your funnel?

355
00:26:10,080 --> 00:26:13,680
Measure the funnel, try to figure out where people drop off, release a few experiments.

356
00:26:14,240 --> 00:26:18,240
We haven't released a bug fix in like a year and a half, like fix all the top bugs.

357
00:26:18,480 --> 00:26:19,520
And let's add a new feature.

358
00:26:19,760 --> 00:26:20,720
Let's add a top feature.

359
00:26:20,880 --> 00:26:23,920
Let's just do the bare minimum, you know, and just see what happens in a year.

360
00:26:24,320 --> 00:26:29,440
So, you know, we went year over year, we went from like 60,000, 70,000 to 120.

361
00:26:29,520 --> 00:26:30,400
It's like, oh, cool.

362
00:26:30,560 --> 00:26:32,400
Like, I can pay, kind of pay myself now.

363
00:26:32,640 --> 00:26:36,480
And that, which I know is a lot of money, but I lived in San Francisco at the peak of the bubble.

364
00:26:36,800 --> 00:26:38,640
And you had a job at Facebook before that.

365
00:26:38,880 --> 00:26:41,120
So your frame of reference on income is a little.

366
00:26:42,400 --> 00:26:44,400
This is a huge thing for indie developers.

367
00:26:44,560 --> 00:27:00,480
And I think part, part of why like, a lot of indie apps just don't ever make sense or get built is like, if you can go make 200 plus at Facebook, like 120, it really is not comparable to like, a lot of indies are like sacrificing to keep the app going.

368
00:27:01,040 --> 00:27:02,400
Well, Indy is still an investment.

369
00:27:02,720 --> 00:27:05,360
I don't think we as an industry actually appreciate that.

370
00:27:05,600 --> 00:27:06,800
Majd made an investment.

371
00:27:07,040 --> 00:27:12,160
I made an investment not by putting cash in the company, but just like living literally from our own savings.

372
00:27:12,320 --> 00:27:14,080
That's still a pretty good investment.

373
00:27:14,960 --> 00:27:19,920
It's not actually sweat, it's actual cash we put in that we didn't get through a job, right?

374
00:27:20,160 --> 00:27:22,960
Missed opportunity, yeah, missed opportunity or opportunity costs, sorry, yeah.

375
00:27:23,120 --> 00:27:28,560
Well, and especially like for Majd and me, like we were high up at Facebook, like you get paid ridiculous amounts of money.

376
00:27:28,720 --> 00:27:34,320
And so especially when you compare it to that, it's a sizeable like missed opportunity or missed, missed opportunity.

377
00:27:34,400 --> 00:27:35,680
I wasn't high up at Facebook, dude.

378
00:27:36,000 --> 00:27:40,080
I was, I was an E5 engineer on like a team nobody cared about.

379
00:27:41,680 --> 00:27:42,640
You were high up at Facebook.

380
00:27:42,960 --> 00:27:44,160
You're a director of like an org.

381
00:27:47,920 --> 00:27:49,600
Take a win when it's handed to you, Mash.

382
00:27:52,160 --> 00:27:54,160
Don't let truth get in the way of getting started.

383
00:27:54,640 --> 00:27:56,080
You gotta work on your reality distortion.

384
00:27:57,760 --> 00:27:58,480
To me, it was like...

385
00:27:59,200 --> 00:28:13,440
I mean, I wasn't convinced with numbers that we like had product market fit because to March's point, like, you know, like we wanted it to be a lot more, but it felt, I mean, for me, most of the time, like anytime I founded a company, built a product, worked on a team, worked on a team, worked with people.

386
00:28:13,600 --> 00:28:15,520
It's more sort of like, does this feel good?

387
00:28:16,160 --> 00:28:18,880
And then in this case, it was like, I really enjoyed working with Marge.

388
00:28:19,120 --> 00:28:20,880
That was like, we had worked as a team.

389
00:28:21,040 --> 00:28:22,000
So there wasn't a question there.

390
00:28:22,160 --> 00:28:23,360
It was like, can I trust the dude?

391
00:28:23,600 --> 00:28:25,040
Does he understand what I'm talking about?

392
00:28:25,280 --> 00:28:27,760
Like, that was like, that was not a question at all.

393
00:28:28,320 --> 00:28:29,440
So that was very comfortable.

394
00:28:30,800 --> 00:28:34,800
And for me, the product was also sort of like a perfect fit because of the reasons I mentioned before.

395
00:28:34,880 --> 00:28:38,000
But also to me, it's like, you know, I liked using the product.

396
00:28:38,320 --> 00:28:52,000
I saw, you know, the foundational thinking that Marge and Matt put into it and that I was part of in the early days that where I was like, man, this like can be so much more, but I also recognizezing it would take a bunch of effort to get there.

397
00:28:52,240 --> 00:28:55,440
And like, yeah, we had to take the small steps to get there.

398
00:28:55,600 --> 00:29:31,040
And I think like, you know, if you'd asked me like before my Facebook days, when I was at Sofa, which was very sort of like, like, founded on the idea of like building products from a pure craft and experience perspective and really sort of taking the public face of Apple and taking that for truth and modeling our company after that, like, I would never have done it or I would never have made it successful because the only thing I would have obsessed about is like making every icon amazing., making a review the best and spending forever polishing a thing that that would never actually be business successful.

399
00:29:31,200 --> 00:29:45,440
But in my years at Facebook, I'd also learned the opposite side of that, like being extremely analytical, being very process driven, like organizing yourself, like instrumenting things so that you know actually what a funnel would look like.

400
00:29:45,520 --> 00:29:53,200
And so for us, it was really like taking those two worlds, like Maj had worked at Apple, I'd worked at Sofa, we both worked at Facebook for us.

401
00:29:53,360 --> 00:29:57,280
It was really like bringing those two worlds together and really like, you know, doing the groundwork.

