00:30:11,600 --> 00:30:15,920
And he's like, this can't make 2,500 to 250,000 a year.

408
00:30:16,000 --> 00:30:16,480
And then it did.

409
00:30:16,960 --> 00:30:23,360
And then like last year, we crossed another huge milestone and like, it blew our minds that it was possible to get there.

410
00:30:23,760 --> 00:30:25,920
But it's taking that step for step thing.

411
00:30:26,160 --> 00:30:30,080
It's also like just hacking up the problem in small pieces and taking one step every day.

412
00:30:30,160 --> 00:30:36,560
And then the hardest part too is having the conviction that the path is worth going on and will lead us to a place and a scale that is worth.

413
00:30:37,440 --> 00:30:38,560
spending the energy to get there.

414
00:30:38,800 --> 00:30:43,520
And the second part of that is saying no to every opportunity along the way to just go chase the money.

415
00:30:44,000 --> 00:30:58,000
There were so many opportunities for us to like turn Darkroom into a story template app and make a heaps more money by optimizing it for SMBs and like increasing our average sell price and like starting a pit, like we knew this in 2017, 2018.

416
00:30:58,320 --> 00:31:01,760
I can't remember exactly what year it was that the stories came out, but like we saw this opportunity.

417
00:31:02,320 --> 00:31:11,040
But like to pursue that meant not to pursue explicitly this vision that we had for mobile photography that to me is a much longer term opportunity.

418
00:31:11,280 --> 00:31:12,400
And so we're not chasing peaks.

419
00:31:13,760 --> 00:31:15,520
high baseline over a long period of time.

420
00:31:16,000 --> 00:31:20,960
There has to be something about your company or your brand that you're doing that's beyond the app you're working on, right?

421
00:31:21,200 --> 00:31:26,960
And if your mission is to, you know, make professionalized iPhone photography, then yeah, building a story.

422
00:31:27,120 --> 00:31:31,280
I mean, maybe there's an angle where a stories tool builds fits into that.

423
00:31:31,680 --> 00:31:35,680
Maybe, but if it's not, it's not, and you can't, you can't, you know, the application needs to be there.

424
00:31:36,000 --> 00:31:42,000
Yeah, but if it's not there, you just, you don't feel bad about not doing it, even if it means, because I think it's, it's, it's that long arc.

425
00:31:42,160 --> 00:31:46,240
So when did you guys, when did you guys make the switch to talk about subscriptions?

426
00:31:46,480 --> 00:31:48,560
When did you guys, when did you guys make the.

427
00:31:49,120 --> 00:31:52,320
switch and throw away your old business model and convert?

428
00:31:53,040 --> 00:31:57,600
And I want to step back just a little bit before that because I we're kind of on this like story arc.

429
00:31:57,920 --> 00:32:09,920
So, uh, so before you decided to switch to subscriptions, you talked about like starting to do the business things that like made sense to build a business, and then that eventually led to start switching to subscriptions.

430
00:32:10,640 --> 00:32:25,600
So, before you answer Jacob's question, take us through like what were the like business things you started implementing even when you still just had inapp purchases that started to get that ball rolling financially in the direction, and then we can jump to the subscription transfer?

431
00:32:26,160 --> 00:32:27,200
There are three components to that.

432
00:32:27,360 --> 00:32:29,280
The first was building features that people wanted.

433
00:32:29,600 --> 00:32:33,760
And so we, our app just didn't have a lot of features that people thought it should have.

434
00:32:34,000 --> 00:32:35,840
And so in many ways that's still the case today.

435
00:32:36,080 --> 00:32:39,040
We're working on that, but like it was really the case in 2016.

436
00:32:39,520 --> 00:32:44,880
And so we invested in ROS support, depth editing support, we added the selective adjustment to frame tool.

437
00:32:44,960 --> 00:32:49,200
Like we added a lot of tools that are core to DarkRoam's use case now, but that didn't exist before.

438
00:32:49,280 --> 00:32:55,280
And so listening to our users and like trying to prioritize what they're asking for and incorporating that into our roadmap was the first big thing that we did.

439
00:32:55,920 --> 00:32:57,680
So that's just from a feature product perspective.

440
00:32:57,920 --> 00:33:01,440
Now from a monetization flow perspective, there were two big things that we did did.

441
00:33:01,600 --> 00:33:08,640
The first was one of the common pieces of feedback that we would hear from people is, I didn't know that like, you had this thing for me to buy.

442
00:33:08,880 --> 00:33:13,840
Like, I just literally didn't know I could spend money on Darkroom.

443
00:33:14,400 --> 00:33:21,600
And we had, we had tried really, really hard to make Darkroom like a smooth experience for our free tier.

444
00:33:21,920 --> 00:33:25,520
And so like, what I tried to say is we removed so much friction, people started slipping.

445
00:33:25,680 --> 00:33:31,360
And so like, we like, one of the things that we tried to do is like, we added an upsell.

446
00:33:31,520 --> 00:33:34,800
Like, we also ran some analytics and we saw that most people make a purchase in their first session.

447
00:33:34,880 --> 00:33:40,160
So we're like, okay, if you don't see an upsell in your first session, like you don't even know the full power of what we're offering for you.

448
00:33:40,320 --> 00:33:46,160
So like, we're not like we're not being pushy, we're just giving you the full information to help you make an informed decision.

449
00:33:46,400 --> 00:33:52,800
And so like, I think we at the time we at the time we were switching away from our mentality of like, oh, like we need to be nice people.

450
00:33:52,960 --> 00:34:01,120
And like, if you ask people to give you money, you're being like a greedy capitalist, which like we had to find the nuance for ourselves between the two.

451
00:34:01,360 --> 00:34:01,760
Yeah, no.

452
00:34:02,000 --> 00:34:04,720
I think that the nuance, finding the nuance was really there.

453
00:34:05,040 --> 00:34:13,040
There's lots of like, I think like the thing to remember for us is like, as we said before, we tried a lot of different things things.

454
00:34:13,280 --> 00:34:15,600
There wasn't like one thing we did and then the revenue doubled.

455
00:34:15,760 --> 00:34:22,640
No, it was like we tried fifteen things and then and all of those cumulatively led to revenue doubling.

456
00:34:22,960 --> 00:34:23,120
Right.

457
00:34:23,360 --> 00:34:33,200
And so it was like making sure we mentioned that like things were for purchase in the first but like here's another one like we had feedback like so so I know there's things I can buy in Darkroom.

458
00:34:33,440 --> 00:34:34,080
Where's the button?

459
00:34:34,880 --> 00:34:34,960
Yeah.

460
00:34:35,600 --> 00:34:35,680
Yeah.

461
00:34:36,160 --> 00:34:37,600
We even passed the screen.

462
00:34:37,920 --> 00:34:39,280
Some people would say not now.

463
00:34:39,600 --> 00:34:41,840
They perused the app and then they couldn't find.

464
00:34:42,240 --> 00:34:43,600
And so it's like, oh, okay.

465
00:34:44,000 --> 00:34:44,000
Yeah.

466
00:34:45,360 --> 00:34:45,520
Okay.

467
00:34:45,840 --> 00:34:48,640
So it was a lot of those kinds.

468
00:34:50,480 --> 00:34:51,760
And those are just two examples.

469
00:34:52,560 --> 00:34:56,160
The third part of that is like what we sold and how much we sold it for also changed.

470
00:34:56,240 --> 00:35:06,000
And so we ended up consolidating all the filter packs and all the tools into a single in our purchase that, you know, when we first consolidated it was six dollars, then we made it eight dollars, and then we made it ten dollars.

471
00:35:06,240 --> 00:35:10,400
And as we kept raising the price, the only thing that changed was our revenue increased.

472
00:35:11,280 --> 00:35:13,360
And so that was a big lesson for us.

473
00:35:13,600 --> 00:35:18,640
And it's funny with monetization lessons, every time you learn a lesson, you're like, man, I wish I knew this lesson three years ago.

474
00:35:18,800 --> 00:35:18,880
Yeah.

475
00:35:19,120 --> 00:35:21,920
Like, you think about all the compound lost revenue, right?

476
00:35:22,160 --> 00:35:23,360
That, yeah, brutal.

477
00:35:23,680 --> 00:35:25,200
Man, it's, it's crazy..

478
00:35:25,360 --> 00:35:30,000
And so eventually a lot of our friends were switching to subscriptions and the subscription thing was well on its way.

479
00:35:30,160 --> 00:35:34,400
And we were so caught up with big infrastructure technical projects.

480
00:35:34,560 --> 00:35:36,640
And by the way, at this point, it was just me and Jasper.

481
00:35:36,880 --> 00:35:40,800
And so when we switched to subscription, we became three people and now we're five people.

482
00:35:41,120 --> 00:35:43,280
So we're still, this is like a really, really small company.

483
00:35:43,520 --> 00:35:51,600
So when I would take on this really big six month long infrastructure project, I would have blinders on and I would be like in a hole.

484
00:35:51,840 --> 00:35:55,520
Meanwhile, the world is changing around me and everyone is making millions on subscriptions.

485
00:35:55,840 --> 00:36:01,040
And so once I came out of my hole and started talking to people again, it was like, oh, subscriptions like our thing.

486
00:36:01,200 --> 00:36:14,960
People are willing to pay for and we had a bunch of our close friends switch their apps over to subscription and they saw big revenue increases and we're like, okay, what we're doing is really not working out for us because at this point we switched to subscription a year ago, almost exactly.

487
00:36:15,120 --> 00:36:20,080
So we're starting to see our annual or recurring revenue coming in, which is brightening up my day.

488
00:36:20,560 --> 00:36:24,560
And that's a big that first year and that first year of renewal really is a big step.

489
00:36:24,960 --> 00:36:30,160
And I imagine y'all's retention rate is on the top end of the industry.

490
00:36:30,400 --> 00:36:30,480
Yeah.

491
00:36:31,040 --> 00:36:31,520
We don't know.

492
00:36:31,680 --> 00:36:32,960
We don't honestly, I don't know.

493
00:36:33,040 --> 00:36:34,400
It's something I want to talk to you guys about.

494
00:36:34,480 --> 00:36:35,520
I want you to write that love first.

495
00:36:36,400 --> 00:36:40,160
Madge and I have a nice thread going about the charts and their limitations.

496
00:36:42,800 --> 00:36:43,440
Good feedback.

497
00:36:43,600 --> 00:36:58,480
But, but, uh, but our, uh, one of the big realizations for us going to subscription was, uh, at this point, 2020, we had been around for five years and there were people who paid us four dollars five years ago who were still getting everything we've worked on since then for free.

498
00:36:58,560 --> 00:37:01,360
And we'll continue to get everything for free because we grandfather people in.

499
00:37:01,760 --> 00:37:04,320
Uh, we gave people the option to subscribe to support us.

500
00:37:04,480 --> 00:37:11,040
But, you know, like those who don't are getting an insane amount of value for four dollars a long time ago.

501
00:37:11,040 --> 00:37:12,960
And that's just not the thing that we also realized.

502
00:37:13,520 --> 00:37:21,600
is we're so incentivized to constantly pump out updates that will get new people in because that's the only way we make money from new people entering the ecosystem.

503
00:37:21,920 --> 00:37:26,240
And like the incentive structure there was making us uncomfortable.

504
00:37:26,800 --> 00:37:29,360
Oh, you're talking about and then when it was a single in-app purchase, right?

505
00:37:29,520 --> 00:37:32,320
Like all your revenue is right up front, right?

506
00:37:32,480 --> 00:37:34,640
So you're all top of funnel focus.

507
00:37:35,040 --> 00:37:40,880
We don't think about our users in this way, but like the incentive structure doesn't matter if you want to, right?

508
00:37:41,120 --> 00:37:42,400
It's incentives right up front.

509
00:37:43,040 --> 00:37:48,640
Once someone gave us the money, once someone came in the door and made the decision to give us money or not, we could like.

510
00:37:48,880 --> 00:37:50,720
the ones they could, we don't care about them anymore.

511
00:37:50,800 --> 00:37:52,320
Like they've already made that snap decision.

512
00:37:52,400 --> 00:37:52,800
And so we can.

513
00:37:53,360 --> 00:37:54,560
We can't tell them about either path they took.

514
00:37:55,040 --> 00:37:58,000
We can tell them about either path because that path is not going to change much after the first session.

515
00:37:58,160 --> 00:37:59,440
And so that's like a really stark way.

516
00:37:59,600 --> 00:38:02,240
That's not how we, that's not our relationship to our customers.

517
00:38:02,560 --> 00:38:09,200
But like that was the incentive structure of a pay like one dollar in app purchase where you only buy it on the first session mostly.

518
00:38:10,080 --> 00:38:19,360
This is what I think a lot of people miss about how well this subscription model aligns consumer value with the developers actually getting paid.

519
00:38:19,920 --> 00:38:23,360
It also really depends on what kind of software business you're running.

520
00:38:23,520 --> 00:38:23,680
Right?

521
00:38:23,840 --> 00:38:30,720
Like there are lots of apps and games out there that like their V1.5 is it, that's the end of the line.

522
00:38:31,200 --> 00:38:31,360
Right.

523
00:38:32,000 --> 00:38:36,320
And for those kinds of businesses and products, a single in a purchase is totally fine.

524
00:38:36,640 --> 00:38:42,080
Like the argument isn't whether that's good or bad, it really depends on like what kind of company and business you build.

525
00:38:42,320 --> 00:38:48,880
And our ambition was like, whew, like photo editing is awesome, but like, man, there's a lot to improve over there.

526
00:38:49,360 --> 00:39:00,000
And like, man, like it's not just like adding a bunch of tools, but it's also like really like re-innovating and rethinking those tools over time and like re-adapting to what comes out this year or next year.

527
00:39:00,640 --> 00:39:22,880
And so like our entire business model is completely or like screwed when you think about single in a purchases because like that doesn't work because to Marge's point, like we're providing tens, if not hundreds of dollars of value for people who paid five bucks or ten bucks once, not to mention they can email you for help at any time, right?

528
00:39:23,200 --> 00:39:27,120
Like probably not totally and expect it, right?

529
00:39:27,360 --> 00:39:27,440
Yeah.

530
00:39:27,840 --> 00:39:27,920
Yeah.

531
00:39:28,400 --> 00:39:28,480
Yeah.

532
00:39:29,440 --> 00:39:29,520
Yeah.

533
00:39:30,800 --> 00:39:44,160
It was quite an interesting switch to go to subscriptions and to what Marge said like, we took it very delicately and we actually like, first kind of tested it out by fully pushing towards a single purchase.

534
00:39:44,480 --> 00:39:47,520
So we already effectively knew what the conversion was going to be.

535
00:39:48,080 --> 00:39:54,800
And even on the product side, a lot of the heavy lifting on sort of like the UI and the flows were already in place.

536
00:39:55,680 --> 00:39:57,200
But you had to bring all that back in, right?

537
00:39:57,360 --> 00:40:02,480
You had all these like Well, yeah, because like, so we basically took the problem and sliced it up into bits.

538
00:40:02,880 --> 00:40:12,240
And so the first one was just simplifying like going from a shopping basket of like five or six things that you could buy to one thing and validating that that could work.

539
00:40:12,400 --> 00:40:12,880
And that worked.

540
00:40:12,880 --> 00:40:15,760
And then increasing the price, eh, that works.

541
00:40:16,000 --> 00:40:18,560
And I was like, okay, now let's switch to subscription.

542
00:40:18,720 --> 00:40:20,240
It's like, oh wait, that works too.

543
00:40:20,400 --> 00:40:20,800
Okay, great.

544
00:40:21,360 --> 00:40:34,560
And I think we did a good job of transitioning to subscription because, like, we were mortified of switching to subscription because we were seeing, like, revolts and mutinies all across the App Store and we didn't, we people celebrated our transition to subscription.

545
00:40:34,640 --> 00:40:35,840
It was pretty, it's pretty nice.

546
00:40:36,320 --> 00:40:37,440
So how did you manage that?

547
00:40:37,760 --> 00:40:44,000
Like the grandfathering, the messaging, like, tell me about, like, how you managed to get that kind of response?

548
00:40:45,920 --> 00:40:48,160
It was mainly predicated on the inside that we.

549
00:40:48,160 --> 00:40:55,040
We that I just mentioned, we only make money from new people downloading the app and deciding to spend ten dollars or not.

550
00:40:55,680 --> 00:41:00,480
So we already are not making money retroactively on people who previously had downloaded the app.

551
00:41:01,120 --> 00:41:16,720
And if we switch to subscription and we have a higher average sale price and we maintain some semblance of conversion after the first year when the revenue starts compounding, we'll be way ahead of where we are now, even if we make zero dollars from anyone who's previously given us money.

552
00:41:17,040 --> 00:41:22,960
And so we basically wrote off like any kind of maintenance costs for people who previously that was the big, big thing.

553
00:41:23,360 --> 00:41:24,480
And also the way we did it was.

554
00:41:25,120 --> 00:41:29,200
I think people felt guilty about how little we charged and how much value they were getting out of Darkroom.

555
00:41:29,520 --> 00:41:43,520
So we added, we added like this sheet that shows if you've previously purchased anything in Darkroom that says, hey, like, even if you bought a filter pack five years ago, we'll give you everything for free, we'll grandfather you into a subscription, but we give you the option to support us.

556
00:41:44,000 --> 00:41:51,360
And like, a fair number of people opt into that, mainly because like they understand, oh, there's like a small team.

557
00:41:51,840 --> 00:41:54,640
And I think one thing, there's time is like a really important component of this.

558
00:41:54,720 --> 00:41:57,200
Like, we're not a new app, we're a six year old app at this point.

559
00:41:57,360 --> 00:41:58,800
And so people know that we're committed to this.

560
00:41:59,040 --> 00:42:00,480
We have a track record at this stage, right?

561
00:42:01,040 --> 00:42:02,240
Yeah, we we've taken no funding.

562
00:42:02,480 --> 00:42:04,000
So people know we're not chasing an exit.

563
00:42:04,160 --> 00:42:05,920
People know we're going to be around for a long time.

564
00:42:06,080 --> 00:42:08,720
And we've demonstrated that, like, tenacity to survive.

565
00:42:09,120 --> 00:42:12,560
And so I think people are more like willing to say, Oh, okay.

566
00:42:12,720 --> 00:42:12,800
Yeah.

567
00:42:12,960 --> 00:42:18,400
Like, if I subscribe to this, they're not going to just go, like, sell it to the next person who offers them, like, X dollars.

568
00:42:19,360 --> 00:42:19,440
Yeah.

569
00:42:19,840 --> 00:42:23,920
In communication of our switch to subscriptions, that was a very big piece of it.

570
00:42:24,560 --> 00:42:27,040
It's like, guys, we're not here to screw around.

571
00:42:27,200 --> 00:42:30,640
We're not that one app that was going to provide one more update and then we're out of here.

572
00:42:30,720 --> 00:42:33,920
And then we're going to cash in for the next ten years on your subscription, right?

573
00:42:34,240 --> 00:42:36,880
It was very clearly like to say, like, no, that's not who we are..

574
00:42:37,360 --> 00:42:42,560
We're actually going to update the app as we have frequently in the past six years.

575
00:42:43,040 --> 00:42:43,120
Yeah.

576
00:42:43,520 --> 00:42:43,680
Right.

577
00:42:44,000 --> 00:42:51,680
And also we added a forever option to allow you to unlock everything forever and price it at two and a half years worth of the annual subscription.

578
00:42:51,920 --> 00:42:58,000
We don't, I don't think we're going to have that option available for a long time, but it provides like a big cash flow upfront for us.

579
00:42:58,320 --> 00:43:01,920
And that cash flow is what allowed us to double the size of our company last year, hire people.

580
00:43:02,400 --> 00:43:02,480
Yeah.

581
00:43:02,800 --> 00:43:08,240
Which, who and those people will allow us to build the features that will make our company worth more.

582
00:43:08,560 --> 00:43:09,040
It's beautiful.

583
00:43:09,200 --> 00:43:10,240
You have the flywheel now, right?

584
00:43:10,320 --> 00:43:11,280
Like you have the flywheel.

585
00:43:11,360 --> 00:43:16,160
You had something with product market fit, like it always starts with something useful, then you build the business.

586
00:43:16,480 --> 00:43:26,480
And then, then when you get this flywhole, now you guys have something that you can build on that you can sustain, that you can then maybe the stories, I mean, as a stupid example, but maybe that opportunity is available to you later.

587
00:43:26,720 --> 00:43:30,400
Like, and you can continue, I mean, it depends on your, it depends on your goals.

588
00:43:30,480 --> 00:43:33,200
You guys talk a lot about this like independence, right?

589
00:43:33,440 --> 00:43:42,560
And this like concept of an indie developer, I think you brought up is, is, uh, maybe a little bit more broad than like the single person working out of a cafe.

590
00:43:42,640 --> 00:43:45,600
I mean, obviously kind of how it started in this story, right?

591
00:43:45,920 --> 00:43:52,160
But, but, uh, but I'm curious, like, what is your., you know, what is your motivation to keep it like that?

592
00:43:52,480 --> 00:44:00,000
And like, what, what, so what's your vision for how you, yeah, how you continue to grow this thing in that, in that, in that, you know, with that ethos, I guess?

593
00:44:00,640 --> 00:44:12,560
So, when in 2014, when I was thinking about starting Darkroom, I, uh, my, uh, conclusion was the opportunity I'm pursuing is not VC scale.

594
00:44:12,960 --> 00:44:19,200
Like if Darkroom is really, really successful, it still would fail as a VC company and I would have to, like, sell it to get return to the investors.

595
00:44:19,360 --> 00:44:27,040
And I think that's because I had been immersed in the Silicon Valley world long enough to understand the mechanics of how VC backed companies work.

596
00:44:27,200 --> 00:44:29,680
And I, you know, like half my friends are VCs.

597
00:44:29,760 --> 00:44:31,680
So like, I have nothing against like the VC model.

598
00:44:32,320 --> 00:44:41,120
Um, but it does have come with a ticking time bomb that like, if you don't grow enough to be able to raise the next round or IPO, then like, what's happening?

599
00:44:41,360 --> 00:44:45,280
Either you buy out the investors or you exit somehow or you get a buyout.

600
00:44:46,160 --> 00:44:46,240
Yeah.

601
00:44:46,720 --> 00:44:46,880
Right.

602
00:44:47,040 --> 00:44:47,280
Exactly.

603
00:44:47,440 --> 00:44:50,720
And so, and so I was like, man, like, this is awesome.

604
00:44:50,880 --> 00:44:51,760
I really want to work on this.

605
00:44:51,920 --> 00:44:55,840
